//
// A VALUE-CONST BINDING REFUSES A WRITE WHATEVER NAME OR ROUTE REACHES IT (`@FR-Const-Value`: a
// binding opted out of every through-write, the rule being about the write's ROOT).  A write
// reaches its binding through a null discharge inside the place (`h.i?.x = …`, loft#1211), through
// a `&` link, a struct view, a loop variable or a discharge target (`@FR-B-Ref-Alias`,
// `@FR-B-View`, `@FR-E-Asgn-Discharge`), and through every append route — vector, keyed, text,
// the `& const` spelling — and each is refused at compile time, whatever lowers it.  Each of the
// 21 expectations is met only by its own refusal: corrupting any one alone fails the file (the
// harness once credited an expectation after a firing one without matching, loft#1261, which is
// why the sections below speak of checking each refusal alone).  Three
// sections, each with its expectations and receipt; the read-only uses of the same shapes run in
// `a-link-a-view-and-a-discharge-of-a-const-value-read-as-the-value.loft`.
//
// ── 1211-a-const-binding-holds-through-an-interior-discharge ──
//
// loft#1211's NEGATIVE half.  `const` binds through a NULL DISCHARGE that sits INSIDE an
// assignment place, exactly as it binds through the dense spelling one field over.
//
// `(Const-Value)` (@FR-Const-Value) opts a binding OUT of every through-write, and the rule
// is about the write's ROOT: `h.i?.x = …` reaches `h` for the same reason `h.i.x = …` does,
// because a discharge changes what a read ANSWERS and not which binding a write travels to.
// So this is enforcement that was missing at a site, not a rule being extended — the target
// `.x` is an interior place (`H-View`) and the write itself stays legal on a non-`const`
// receiver, which `1211b` scores.
//
// The defect: `lhs_base_var` walks a place to find the binding a write reaches and had no arm
// for either shape a discharge lowers to, so a place ROOTED in one answered `u16::MAX` — no
// binding at all — and `validate_write` had nothing to check.  A `const` parameter was mutated
// in silence and the CALLER saw the new value, on both backends, with no diagnostic.
//
// The dense control — `i: Ic1211` and `h.i.x = 99`, refused throughout — is what named the
// discharge as the cause rather than `const` itself, and it is deliberately NOT a cell here.
// Scored in this file it makes the file exit 1 on the pre-fix build too, which leaves
// `falsify.sh` reading INERT on every channel it has (measured: `1|0|none|none` both trees,
// both backends) and moves the whole answer into a diagnostic COUNT only `tests/wrap.rs` can
// see.  It is not needed for what it was there for either: a build where `const` dies outright
// leaves this file's own `@EXPECT_ERROR` unmatched, so the harness fails it anyway.  Measured
// by hand instead, both backends, on the pre-fix build and on this one — "Cannot modify const
// parameter 'h'" in all four runs.
//
// In its own file because a firing `@EXPECT_ERROR` stops the file before it runs; the writes
// that must still go through are `1211b-a-place-behind-an-interior-discharge-is-written.loft`.
//
// @falsified-at: c3226d07 — interpret exit 0 -> 1, native exit 0 -> 1.  On the control the file
//   compiles and RUNS with no diagnostic at all: the `const` parameter is mutated and the run
//   reports nothing, which is the defect.  `make falsify` reads that pair but cannot render a
//   verdict for an annotation-scored file — a passing `@EXPECT_ERROR` guard exits 1, which its
//   exit channel reads as "THIS TREE IS NOT CLEAN".  The gating channel is `check_diagnostics`
//   in `tests/wrap.rs`: the `@EXPECT_ERROR` above goes unmatched on the control, because there
//   is nothing to match.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal are equal on both trees. Falsify prints only
//   the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both.
//   The native side was not scored in this sweep.
//
// ── a-link-a-view-and-a-discharge-of-a-const-value-are-read-only-refused ──
//
// `@FR-Const-Value` crossed with `@FR-B-Ref-Alias`, `@FR-B-View` and `@FR-E-Asgn-Discharge`: a
// value-const binding is read-only THROUGH EVERY NAME that names its place.  A `&` link
// (B-Ref-Alias) to a field of a const parameter or a value-const local is refused a write, an
// append included; a struct view (B-View) — a field projection, an element bound to a local,
// a `for` loop variable — is refused a write; and a compound assignment whose target is a
// DISCHARGE (`place? op= e`, E-Asgn-Discharge) writes `place`, so a discharge rooted in a
// const binding is refused whether it is the whole target, a nested field's, a path inside a
// nullable member, or reached through a view.  The read-only uses of the same shapes, and
// the discharge writes on a writable binding, run in
// `a-link-a-view-and-a-discharge-of-a-const-value-read-as-the-value.loft`.
//
// Every cell held on both backends when written; the ledger rows are in
// doc/claude/RULE_CROSSINGS-history.md.
//
// @falsified-at: none — the walk found no defect, so no earlier build answers wrong.
// CHANNEL: the expectations and the exit code; each refusal was also checked alone
//   (a later `@EXPECT_ERROR` can be credited without matching, loft#1261).
//
// ── const-binds-through-every-append-route ──
//
// `const` binds whatever route the append takes.
//
// @FR-Const-Value says the value behind a value-const name is read-only and EVERY
// through-write is rejected.  The check used to live inside the lowering routes — one copy
// in the vector builder, one in the keyed builder, one in each text path — so it was only
// ever as complete as each route's own target-shape test, and a shape a route declined fell
// through unchecked.  Two shapes did, and both mutated the CALLER on both backends while
// the parameter said `const`:
//
//   * `p: & const vector<T>` — the vector route destructured `f_type.base()` against
//     `Type::Vector`, and `base()` peels `Optional` but not `RefVar`, so the reference
//     spelling never reached the check.
//   * `p: const hash<R[k]>` (and `sorted`, `index`) — the keyed append routes carried no
//     const check at all.
//
// The cure is one guard ahead of the route dispatch, because whether a write is allowed is
// a property of the BINDING and never of the route that lowers it.  These cells are the
// four that were silent plus the ones that were already right, so a future route added
// below the guard cannot re-open the hole for one kind while the others stay closed.
//
// @falsified-at: 3477a51f5 — the commit before the guard was hoisted.  Measured CELL BY
//   CELL, each run alone, because `make falsify` cannot score this file whole: it reports
//   `expect 8/8` and INERT on the very build the defect was measured on, since every
//   `@EXPECT_ERROR` after a firing one is credited without matching (loft#1261).  Run
//   alone, each cell answers:
//
//     a_reference_const_vector_refuses_append   control FAIL -> here PASS
//     a_const_hash_refuses_append               control FAIL -> here PASS
//     a_const_sorted_refuses_append             control FAIL -> here PASS
//     a_const_index_refuses_append              control FAIL -> here PASS
//     a_const_vector_refuses_append             control PASS -> here PASS   (a lock)
//     a_const_text_refuses_append               control PASS -> here PASS   (a lock)
//     a_reference_const_text_refuses_append     control PASS -> here PASS   (a lock)
//     a_const_keyed_refuses_an_element_write    control PASS -> here PASS   (a lock)
//     a_value_const_local_refuses_append        control PASS -> here PASS   (a lock)
//     a_binding_const_scalar_refuses_compound_assignment
//                                               control PASS -> here PASS   (a lock)
//
//   The four that move are the defect: on the control they do not merely lose a
//   diagnostic, they compile and append into the CALLER's collection.  The six locks are
//   the routes that were already right, kept here so one guard answers for all of them —
//   the two local ones because the Store Locks chapter tells a reader to write exactly
//   those and states in a comment that they do not compile, which nothing else checks.
//
//   The allowance cells are the other half and are not decoration: `Const-Bind` leaves the
//   contents of a binding-const local mutable, so a guard that simply refused more would
//   pass every refusal cell here while breaking the builder idiom.  They RUN, and a
//   deliberately broken assertion in one fails the file — checked, so they are not being
//   skipped the way the trailing refusal cells are.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see GUARDS.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.
//   HOLDS: the WHOLE-FILE verdict is unusable, not merely inert — falsify reports `expect
//   8/8` and INERT on the very build the defect was measured on, because every
//   `@EXPECT_ERROR` after a firing one is credited without matching (loft#1261). Nothing
//   about this file's channels can be read off a bulk row. Score it CELL BY CELL, each run
//   alone, as the receipt above records.

struct Ic1211 { x: integer }
struct Nc1211 { i: Ic1211? }

// The cell.  A discharge INTERIOR to the place, on a `const` receiver.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'h1211n'
fn n1211_touch(h1211n: const Nc1211) {
  h1211n.i?.x = 99;
}

fn test_a_const_binding_holds_through_an_interior_discharge() {
  n1211_touch(Nc1211 { i: Ic1211 { x: 1 } });
}

struct In { k: integer, n: integer? }
struct S { inner: In, items: vector<In>, v: vector<integer>, m: integer? }
struct O { i: In?, tag: integer }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify 'd': it is a view of const parameter 'p'
fn a1_a_link_appended(p: const S) -> integer { d = &p.v; d += [1]; len(d) }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify 'd': it is a view of const parameter 'p'
fn a2_a_link_field_written(p: const S) -> integer { d = &p.inner; d.k = 4; d.k }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify 'd': it is a view of const variable 'x'
fn a3_a_link_into_a_const_local(q: S) -> integer { x: const S = q; d = &x.v; d[0] = 3; len(x.v) }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify 'e': it is a view of const parameter 'p'
fn v1_a_member_view_written(p: const S) -> integer { e = p.inner; e.k = 1; e.k }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify 'e': it is a view of const parameter 'p'
fn v2_a_loop_variable_written(p: const S) -> integer { for e in p.items { e.k = 1; } len(p.items) }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify 'e': it is a view of const parameter 'p'
fn v3_an_element_view_compound(p: const S) -> integer { e = p.items[0]; e.k += 2; e.k }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'p'
fn d1_a_discharge_target(p: const S) -> integer { p.m? += 1; p.m ?? 0 }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'h'
fn d2_a_path_through_a_nullable_member(h: const O) -> integer { h.i?.k += 1; h.tag }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'p'
fn d3_a_nested_discharge_target(p: const S) -> integer { p.inner.n? += 2; p.inner.k }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify 'e': it is a view of const parameter 'p'
fn d4_a_discharge_through_a_view(p: const S) -> integer { e = p.inner; e.n? += 2; e.k }

struct CRow { k: text, n: integer }

// ── the four that were silent ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A `&` reference parameter is still value-const: the append must not reach the caller.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'cv'
fn a_reference_const_vector_refuses_append(cv: & const vector<integer>) {
  cv += [9];
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'ch'
fn a_const_hash_refuses_append(ch: const hash<CRow[k]>) {
  ch += CRow { k: "b", n: 2 };
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'cs'
fn a_const_sorted_refuses_append(cs: const sorted<CRow[k]>) {
  cs += CRow { k: "b", n: 2 };
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'ci'
fn a_const_index_refuses_append(ci: const index<CRow[k]>) {
  ci += CRow { k: "b", n: 2 };
}

// ── the routes that were already right, kept so one guard answers for all of them ────

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'pv'
fn a_const_vector_refuses_append(pv: const vector<integer>) {
  pv += [9];
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'pt'
fn a_const_text_refuses_append(pt: const text) {
  pt += "x";
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'rt'
fn a_reference_const_text_refuses_append(rt: & const text) {
  rt += "x";
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'pe'
fn a_const_keyed_refuses_an_element_write(pe: const hash<CRow[k]>) {
  pe["a"].n = 5;
}

// A value-const LOCAL is the same rule reached without a parameter, and it is what the
// Store Locks chapter tells a reader to write when they want frozen contents.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const variable 'cl'
fn a_value_const_local_refuses_append() {
  cl: const vector<integer> = [1, 2, 3];
  cl += [4];
}

// Scalar collapse: a binding-const whole number has no interior, so `+=` rewrites the
// whole value exactly as `=` does and both are refused.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const variable 'cn'
fn a_binding_const_scalar_refuses_compound_assignment() {
  const cn = 1;
  cn += 1;
}

// ── what `const` still ALLOWS, so the guard cannot pass by refusing everything ────────

// Binding-const freezes the SLOT and leaves the contents mutable: this is the builder
// shape, and it is the idiom a wider guard would break.
fn a_binding_const_local_still_grows_in_place() {
  const cx = [1, 2, 3];
  cx += [4];
  cx[0] = 9;
  assert(len(cx) == 4, "binding-const local appends: {len(cx)}");
  assert(cx[0] == 9, "binding-const local takes an element write: {cx[0]}");
}

// Value-const freezes the value and leaves the slot re-pointable.
fn a_value_const_local_still_repoints() {
  cy: const vector<integer> = [1, 2, 3];
  cy = [7, 8];
  assert(len(cy) == 2, "value-const local rebinds: {len(cy)}");
}

// A value-const PARAMETER of compound type re-points the callee's own slot, and the
// caller's value is untouched — the read-only borrow, not a frozen binding.
fn cf_repoint(cz: const vector<integer>) -> integer {
  cz = [7, 8, 9];
  len(cz)
}

fn a_value_const_parameter_still_repoints_its_own_slot() {
  cw = [1];
  assert(cf_repoint(cw) == 3, "callee re-points its own slot");
  assert(len(cw) == 1, "caller is untouched: {len(cw)}");
}
